## std::array — Array (formerly `Vec`) method surface, routed through FFI alias.
##
## #06.8 T#14: every method here was a `Vec[...]_<m>` /
## `Array[...]_<m>` entry in the `name.starts_with("Array")` arm of
## `compiler/ruxen_core/src/codegen/runtime_table/mod.rs` until T#21's
## namespace-anchor mechanism was extended in T#14 to cover collection
## type names. `Array`/`Vec` is NOT in `register_builtins`'s type-scope
## map — `let x: Array[Int]` resolves through a hardcoded match arm in
## `resolve_type_expr` that builds `Ty::Array(Int)` directly. The
## anchor branch for these names creates a `DefId` for bookkeeping but
## does NOT insert into type-scope, so the hardcoded type resolver
## remains authoritative.
##
## ## Generic-stripping at the call site (T#17 mechanism)
##
## MIR mangles the surface generic in the call: `Vec[Int]_push`,
## `Array[String]_clone`, etc. The anchor `class Array` shell
## registers methods under the parent NAME, so the alias-map key is
## `Array_push` / `Array_clone`. The lookup site in
## `mir/lower/expr/method_call.rs` peels the `[...]` segment from the
## mangled callee and retries with the generic-stripped key — that
## same fallback added for Option/Result in T#17 makes the entries
## below load-bearing.
##
## ## Aliased entries kept in runtime_table
##
## Multiple Ruxen names sharing a single C symbol trip the E0722
## conflict check in `register_class_lib_method` (the check compares
## explicit param lists; instance-vs-class shape differences look
## like incompatible sigs even when wire-compatible). For collections
## the aliased clusters are:
##
##   * `get_mut`, `get_var` → `ruxen_vec_get_opt` (same as `get`)
##   * `into_iter`, `iter_mut`, `to_vec`, `enumerate`, `as_slice`
##     → `ruxen_iter_to_vec` (same as `iter`)
##
## We declare ONE Ruxen name per cluster (the canonical surface
## spelling) and leave the aliases in `runtime_table` until the E0722
## check is taught to compare at the wire level. The migration is
## therefore mechanically incomplete by a handful of low-traffic
## aliases — same shape as the `String_clone` outlier from T#13.
class Array
  lib "runtime/vec.c"
    def self.new as "ruxen_vec_new" -> Int
    def self.with_capacity as "ruxen_vec_with_capacity"(cap: Int) -> Int
    def self.from_iter as "ruxen_vec_from_iter"(items: Int) -> Int
    def len as "ruxen_vec_len" -> Int
    def capacity as "ruxen_vec_capacity" -> Int
    def is_empty as "ruxen_vec_is_empty" -> Bool
    def get as "ruxen_vec_get_opt"(i: Int) -> Int
    def first as "ruxen_vec_first" -> Int
    def last as "ruxen_vec_last" -> Int
    def count as "ruxen_vec_count" -> Int
    def contains as "ruxen_vec_contains_int"(x: Int) -> Bool
    def sum as "ruxen_vec_sum" -> Int
    def clone as "ruxen_vec_clone" -> Int
    def join as "ruxen_vec_join"(sep: &String) -> String
    def iter as "ruxen_iter_to_vec" -> Int
    def each as "ruxen_vec_each" -> Int
    def push as "ruxen_vec_push"(x: Int)
    def pop as "ruxen_vec_pop" -> Int
    def clear as "ruxen_vec_clear"
    def truncate as "ruxen_vec_truncate"(n: Int)
    def swap as "ruxen_vec_swap"(i: Int, j: Int)
    def insert as "ruxen_vec_insert"(i: Int, x: Int)
    def remove as "ruxen_vec_remove"(i: Int) -> Int
    def extend as "ruxen_vec_extend"(other: Int)
    def dedup as "ruxen_vec_dedup"
    def reverse as "ruxen_vec_reverse" -> Int
    def sort as "ruxen_vec_sort" -> Int
  end
end
